# Lore for the in-game People window, part 1: (history, living culture) per people. Real history up to the
# early twenty-first century; the 2240 layer is added by peoples_story.py.
LORE = {
'anglo_am': ("They grew out of the English, Scots-Irish and German settlers of the thirteen colonies, who won independence in 1783, pushed west across the continent and absorbed wave after wave of European immigrants until the United States became the strongest power of the twentieth century.",
             "Their culture is the one the world knew best for two centuries: Thanksgiving turkey and the Fourth of July, barbecue arguments between Texas and Carolina, country music, rock and roll, baseball and gridiron football, and a stubborn faith in local self-government and the town meeting."),
'hannorth': ("Their plain was the cradle of Chinese civilisation: the Shang and Zhou ruled from the Yellow River valley, Beijing was the seat of the Yuan, Ming and Qing empires, and Manchuria was opened to Han farmers in great migrations in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.",
             "Theirs is a wheat country, not a rice one: dumplings at the New Year, hand-pulled noodles, steamed buns and Peking duck. Beijing opera, xiangsheng comic dialogues and the spoken Mandarin of the capital, the standard for a billion speakers, come from them."),
'brazilian': ("Portugal claimed Brazil in 1500 and built it on sugar, gold and the labour of four million enslaved Africans; it became an empire of its own in 1822 and a republic in 1889, and Italians, Germans, Japanese, Arabs and others made it one of the most mixed nations on Earth.",
              "They live for Carnival, samba and bossa nova, football played as an art, feijoada on Saturday and churrasco on Sunday. Their saints' festivals in June, telenovelas and the easy warmth of their speech carry from the favelas of Rio to the farms of Mato Grosso."),
'hindustani': ("Their speech grew in the Delhi region from the meeting of old Indian vernaculars with the Persian of the Delhi Sultanate and the Mughal court, and split in the twentieth century into Hindi in Devanagari and Urdu in Persian script, the national languages of India and Pakistan.",
               "Theirs is the culture of the Ganges plain and its cities: Diwali lamps and Holi colours, Eid feasts in the old quarters, ghazals and qawwali, Bollywood songs, kebabs of Lucknow and the chaat of Delhi street stalls, and the holy ghats of Varanasi."),
'mexican': ("They were born from the conquest of the Aztec and other Mesoamerican states by Spain in 1521, from the mixing of Spaniards, Native peoples and Africans over three colonial centuries, and from a long struggle for nationhood: independence in 1821, the Reform, and the Revolution of 1910.",
            "Maize is still at the heart of their table, as tortillas, tamales and pozole, with mole, chiles and tacos al pastor. They honour the dead on the Dia de Muertos, gather for the Virgin of Guadalupe on 12 December, and love mariachi, rancheras, lucha libre and football."),
'japanese': ("Their islands developed a court culture at Nara and Kyoto, centuries of samurai rule under shoguns, and over two hundred years of near-isolation under the Tokugawa, before the Meiji Restoration of 1868 turned Japan into a modern industrial empire, defeated in 1945 and rebuilt as a rich democracy.",
             "They keep the New Year at shrines and temples, view the cherry blossom in spring, and honour their ancestors at Obon. Their kitchen of rice, fish, miso and dashi, their tea ceremony, kabuki, manga, anime and an exacting sense of craft and courtesy are known around the world."),
'russian': ("They descend from the East Slavs of Kievan Rus, baptised in 988; Muscovy threw off Mongol rule, grew into a Tsarist empire spanning eleven time zones, became the heart of the Soviet Union after 1917 and the Russian Federation after 1991.",
            "They are a people of long winters and great literature: Pushkin, Tolstoy and Dostoevsky, Tchaikovsky's ballets, Orthodox Easter with kulich and painted eggs, the dacha garden, the banya, borscht and pelmeni, and New Year's night as the warmest feast of the year."),
'colombian': ("Their lands were the heart of the Viceroyalty of New Granada; Simon Bolivar's wars freed them from Spain, and Gran Colombia briefly united them before Colombia, Venezuela and Ecuador went their own ways, joined by oil, coffee and a shared, turbulent history.",
              "They dance cumbia, vallenato, salsa and joropo, eat arepas every morning, drink strong coffee from the Andean slopes, and gather for the Carnival of Barranquilla and the flower fair of Medellin. Gabriel Garcia Marquez wrote their magic into the world's literature."),
'french': ("They grew from Gaul, conquered by Rome and later by the Franks, into the kingdom of the Capetians, the absolute monarchy of Louis XIV, the Revolution of 1789 and Napoleon's empire, and then the republic that spread its language and ideas from Quebec to Indochina.",
           "The French made an art of the table: bread, wine and cheese, bistros and great kitchens, the long Sunday lunch. Bastille Day, the Tour de France, cafes, cinema, fashion and a passionate love of argument, philosophy and the French language itself define them."),
'turkish': ("Oghuz Turks came into Anatolia after the battle of Manzikert in 1071; the Ottoman dynasty built from there an empire that took Constantinople in 1453 and ruled the Balkans and the Arab world for centuries, until Ataturk founded the modern Turkish Republic in 1923.",
            "They are known for hospitality and the tea glass that is always refilled, for kebab, meze, baklava and Turkish coffee, the bazaar and the hammam. Folk songs with the saz, Sufi music, wedding dances, football and the two feasts of Ramazan and Kurban mark their year."),
'persian': ("Their history runs from the Achaemenid empire of Cyrus and Darius through the Parthians and Sassanids, the Islamic conquest and the Persian renaissance, the Safavids who made Iran Shia, to the constitutional revolution of 1906 and the revolution of 1979.",
            "Nowruz, the new year at the spring equinox, with its haft-sin table, is their greatest feast. They revere the poets Ferdowsi, Hafez, Rumi and Saadi, weave the finest carpets on Earth, and cook saffron rice with tahdig, slow stews and pomegranate dishes in walled gardens."),
'german': ("They descend from the Germanic tribes Rome never conquered; the Holy Roman Empire bound their many states loosely for a thousand years, Prussia united them in 1871, two world wars and the Nazi crimes broke them, and they reunited peacefully in 1990.",
           "They are a people of music and order: Bach, Beethoven and Brahms, Christmas markets and Advent wreaths, beer halls, bread in hundreds of kinds, sausages and Sunday cake. Carnival on the Rhine, Oktoberfest in Bavaria, clubs for everything and a great love of forests and hiking."),
'hansw': ("Han settlers moved into Sichuan, Yunnan and Guizhou over two thousand years, mixing with the local peoples; the region was the Kingdom of Shu in the Three Kingdoms age and China's wartime capital at Chongqing in the 1930s and 1940s.",
          "Their cooking is the fiery Sichuan and Chongqing table of hot pot, mapo tofu and numbing peppercorn. They play mahjong in teahouses, watch Sichuan opera with its face-changing masters, and speak Southwestern Mandarin with a soft, slow lilt."),
'rioplatense': ("Spain's Viceroyalty of the Rio de la Plata became Argentina and Uruguay after 1810; between 1870 and 1930 millions of Italians and Spaniards poured into Buenos Aires and Montevideo, making them some of the most European cities of the Americas.",
                "They drink mate from a gourd at all hours, grill beef at the Sunday asado, dance and sing the tango, and live and die by football. Their Spanish uses vos and sounds half Italian, and they argue about Borges, Maradona and Messi with equal passion."),
'spanish': ("Their land was Roman Hispania, then Visigothic, then for centuries largely Muslim al-Andalus, before the Christian kingdoms completed the Reconquista in 1492, the same year Columbus sailed; Spain then built an empire that spread its language across the Americas.",
            "Spaniards live late and in the street: tapas and long dinners, paella, jamon and tortilla, flamenco in Andalusia, fiestas for every town's patron saint, the Holy Week processions of Seville and the bulls and festivals of Pamplona."),
'korean': ("They have lived on their peninsula for millennia; the Three Kingdoms gave way to Goryeo and the long Joseon dynasty, whose King Sejong invented the Hangul alphabet in 1443, before Japanese colonisation, the division of 1945 and the Korean War.",
           "They keep Seollal and Chuseok with ancestral rites and rice cakes, eat kimchi with every meal, love barbecue, noodles and soju, and gave the world K-pop and Korean film. Respect for elders, hard study and fierce national pride run deep."),
'canadian': ("They descend from the Loyalists who fled the American Revolution, from British and Irish settlers, and from the immigrants of every continent who settled the prairies and the cities after Confederation in 1867.",
             "They are a people of hockey and lakes, Tim Hortons coffee and maple syrup, Canada Day fireworks and the cottage in summer. Polite, plain-spoken and proud of their mosaic of origins, they define themselves as much by what they are not as by what they are."),
'min': ("Their Min dialects branched off very early from Old Chinese and are among the most distinct in China; from Fujian's ports their sailors and traders went out to Taiwan, the Philippines, Malaya and Indonesia, becoming the core of the Chinese diaspora.",
        "They are tea people above all, makers of oolong and connoisseurs of gongfu tea. They worship the sea goddess Mazu, build earthen ancestral halls, cook rich seafood soups and are famous across Southeast Asia as merchants and clan associations."),
'gulf': ("Their peninsula was the birthplace of Islam in the seventh century, with Mecca and Medina in the Hejaz; the Saudi state rose in Najd in the eighteenth century, united most of Arabia by 1932, and was transformed by oil.",
         "They keep coffee with cardamom and dates for every guest, love falconry, camel racing and Nabati poetry, and gather for long evenings in the majlis. Kabsa rice and lamb feed families at Eid, and the pilgrimage to Mecca brings the whole Muslim world to their doors every year."),
'marathi': ("Their Marathi literature began in the thirteenth century with poet-saints of the bhakti movement; in the seventeenth century Shivaji founded the Maratha state, which broke Mughal power and ruled much of India until the British wars.",
            "They celebrate Ganesh Chaturthi with ten days of processions and drums, walk barefoot on the pilgrimage to Pandharpur, eat vada pav, puran poli and spicy misal, and love the theatre and film of Mumbai, which they consider very much their own city."),
'cantonese': ("The Pearl River delta was long China's southern window on the world: Guangzhou traded with Arabs, Persians and Europeans, the Opium Wars began there, and Cantonese emigrants built Chinatowns from San Francisco to Sydney.",
              "They are famous cooks: dim sum and yum cha, roast goose and char siu, slow soups and the freshest seafood. Cantonese opera and Canto-pop, lion dances and their own nine-tone language, older in sound than Mandarin, set them apart."),
'wu': ("The Wu lands around Lake Tai and the Yangtze delta were for a thousand years China's richest region: Suzhou and Hangzhou were proverbial paradises, and Shanghai became in the twentieth century the greatest modern city of Asia.",
       "They are known for the canal towns and classical gardens of Suzhou, silk and Longjing tea, Kunqu opera, Shaoxing wine, soup dumplings and sweet-savoury cooking, and a refined, soft-spoken speech the rest of China finds hard to follow."),
'gulf_placeholder': ("", ""),
'italian': ("They are heirs of Rome and of the city-states of the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, Florence, Venice, Genoa and Milan, which gave the world banking, perspective painting and opera; Italy was united only in 1861, under the house of Savoy.",
            "Their life turns on the family table: pasta, pizza, regional cooking fiercely defended town by town, espresso standing at the bar. Opera, Catholic feasts and processions, fashion and design, Ferrari and football, and an art of living called la dolce vita."),
'quebecois': ("New France was founded by Samuel de Champlain at Quebec in 1608; after the British conquest of 1760 its sixty thousand settlers held on to their language and faith for two centuries, and the Quiet Revolution of the 1960s made them a modern, secular nation.",
              "They celebrate the Saint-Jean-Baptiste on 24 June, gather at the sugar shack every spring for maple taffy, eat poutine and tourtiere, and sing chansonniers and folk songs. Their motto is Je me souviens, I remember, and they guard their French fiercely."),
'maghrebi': ("Arab armies brought Islam to North Africa in the seventh century and Arab tribes settled it over the following centuries, mixing with the Berber peoples; the region gave rise to the Almoravid and Almohad empires and later fell under Ottoman and then French rule.",
             "Couscous on Fridays, mint tea poured from high, tagines, harira at the end of Ramadan, and the music of rai, chaabi and Andalusian orchestras belong to them. The medinas of Fez, Tunis and Algiers, their souks and hammams, are among the oldest living cities in the world."),
'ukrainian': ("They descend from the East Slavs of Kievan Rus; the Cossack Hetmanate of the seventeenth century gave them a national myth, and after centuries under Polish, Russian and Soviet rule they won an independent state in 1991, which they defended in war.",
              "They are a people of black earth and wheat, of borscht, varenyky and salo, embroidered vyshyvanka shirts and painted pysanka eggs at Easter. Their poet is Taras Shevchenko, their songs are long and melancholy, and their bread and salt welcome any guest."),
'punjabi': ("The land of five rivers has been the gateway of every invasion of India; Guru Nanak founded the Sikh faith there in the fifteenth century, Ranjit Singh's Sikh empire ruled it until 1849, and the partition of 1947 split it between India and Pakistan.",
            "They are farmers, soldiers and emigrants famous for their energy: bhangra dancing, Vaisakhi harvest festivals, the Golden Temple's free kitchen that feeds anyone, butter chicken, sarson da saag with makki di roti and lassi in tall glasses."),
'egyptian': ("Their civilisation is among the oldest on Earth: the pharaohs built the pyramids nearly five thousand years ago; Egypt was later Greek, Roman, Christian and, after 641, Arab and Muslim, and Cairo became the cultural capital of the Arab world.",
             "Life follows the Nile: ful medames and koshari, fiteer, sugar cane juice, crowded cafes with shisha, Ramadan lanterns and mulid festivals for the saints. Umm Kulthum's songs, Egyptian cinema and a famous sense of humour are known across the Arab world."),
'anglo_au': ("Britain founded New South Wales as a penal colony in 1788; free settlers, the gold rushes of the 1850s and later immigration from all over Europe and Asia built a federation in 1901, at great cost to the Aboriginal peoples.",
             "They love the beach, the barbecue and sport of every kind, cricket and the football codes above all. They keep Anzac Day at dawn, mock pretension with dry humour, call each other mate, and spread Vegemite on their toast."),
'mongol': ("Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes in 1206 and his heirs built the largest contiguous land empire in history, from Korea to Hungary; after its fall the Mongols lived as herders under Manchu rule until Outer Mongolia won independence in the twentieth century.",
           "They are horse people: the Naadam games of wrestling, archery and horse racing, the felt ger, airag of fermented mare's milk, buuz dumplings at Tsagaan Sar, throat singing and the horse-head fiddle, and a deep reverence for the open sky."),
'andean': ("The Spanish conquest of the Inca empire in the 1530s created a mixed society in the Andes; mestizo townspeople became the backbone of Peru, Ecuador and Bolivia after independence from Spain in the 1820s.",
           "Their tables mix the Andes and Spain: ceviche, potatoes in hundreds of varieties, quinoa and roast guinea pig, chicha and pisco. They dance the marinera and huayno, and the Catholic feasts of the highland towns blend saints with older mountain spirits."),
'telugu': ("Their land saw the Satavahana and Kakatiya dynasties and the great Vijayanagara empire, then the Qutb Shahis and the Nizams of Hyderabad, the richest of India's princely states; Telugu literature flowered from the eleventh century on.",
           "They eat the spiciest food in India: Andhra meals on banana leaves, Hyderabadi biryani, pickles and gongura. They celebrate Sankranti with kites and Ugadi as new year, love classical Kuchipudi dance, Carnatic music and a huge, star-mad film industry."),
'levantine': ("The Levant is one of the oldest settled regions on Earth, home of Phoenicians, Arameans, and the cities of Damascus, Aleppo, Beirut and Jerusalem; it became Arab-speaking after the seventh century and was divided into modern states after the Ottoman empire fell.",
              "They are famous for hospitality and food: mezze of hummus, tabbouleh and kibbeh, manakish at breakfast, olive oil and za'atar. Dabke dances at weddings, Fairuz in the morning and coffee with cardamom bind together Muslims, Christians and Druze."),
'kongo': ("The Kingdom of Kongo was a great state of the fourteenth to seventeenth centuries whose kings became Christian; the Atlantic slave trade and then Belgian and French colonial rule devastated the region before independence in 1960.",
          "Their Lingala and Kikongo music, from the Congolese rumba of Franco to soukous and ndombolo, made Africa dance. Kinshasa's sapeurs dress like dandies, families gather over fufu, pondu and grilled fish, and the churches overflow with choirs."),
'polish': ("Poland was baptised in 966; its Commonwealth with Lithuania was once Europe's largest state, but it was partitioned out of existence in 1795, reborn in 1918, ravaged in the Second World War and freed from communism in 1989 by the Solidarity movement.",
           "They keep Wigilia on Christmas Eve with twelve meatless dishes, eat pierogi, bigos and zurek, light candles on graves at All Saints, and are passionately Catholic and patriotic. Chopin, Copernicus and Marie Curie are their pride."),
'sudanese': ("The Nile valley of Sudan was the land of ancient Kush and Meroe, whose kings once ruled Egypt; Christian Nubian kingdoms lasted until the fifteenth century, and Arab tribes and Islam spread through the region before the Mahdist state and Anglo-Egyptian rule.",
             "They are famous for generosity and long evenings of conversation, for ful, kisra and gurasa, sweet tea and coffee brewed in the jebena. Sufi zikr ceremonies, Nubian songs and henna for weddings mark their year."),
'thai': ("Tai peoples founded Sukhothai in the thirteenth century and then Ayutthaya, one of the great trading cities of Asia; after its fall in 1767 the Chakri dynasty ruled from Bangkok, and Siam was the only Southeast Asian kingdom never colonised by Europe.",
         "They are Theravada Buddhists who make merit at the temple, splash water at the Songkran new year and float lights at Loy Krathong. Their cooking of sour, sweet, salty and hot, Muay Thai boxing and a gentle code of respect and saving face are famous."),
'malay': ("The Malay world was shaped by the trading empires of Srivijaya and the Sultanate of Malacca, whose conversion to Islam in the fifteenth century spread the faith across the islands; Portuguese, Dutch and British rule followed before Malaysia's independence.",
          "They live by adat, custom, and Islam: open houses at Hari Raya, nasi lemak and rendang, kampung houses on stilts, silat martial arts, batik and songket weaving, and pantun verses traded at weddings."),
'centroamerican': ("Their isthmus was home to Maya cities before the Spanish conquest; the five republics of Guatemala, Honduras, El Salvador, Nicaragua and Costa Rica broke from Spain in 1821 and briefly formed a federation before going separate ways.",
                   "They eat pupusas, baleadas, gallo pinto and tamales wrapped in banana leaves, celebrate patron saints with processions and fireworks, and dance to marimba and punta. Coffee, bananas and close family ties bind the region together."),
'first_nations': ("Hundreds of nations lived in North America for thousands of years before Europeans arrived; disease, war, treaties and the reservation and residential-school systems took a terrible toll, but their nations survived and revived in the twentieth century.",
                  "Powwows with drum circles and jingle dancers, sweat lodges and sacred pipes, beadwork, quillwork and frybread are shared across many nations. Each people keeps its own language, clans, ceremonies and stories of how the world began."),
'kazakh': ("The Kazakh Khanate formed in the fifteenth century from Turkic and Mongol tribes of the steppe, organised in three hordes; Russia absorbed them in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, and the famine of the 1930s killed a third of the nation before independence in 1991.",
           "They are steppe people of the horse and the yurt: beshbarmak of boiled meat and noodles, kumis, the kokpar game on horseback, eagle hunting, and the dombra lute. Nauryz in spring and lavish hospitality for guests are sacred to them."),
'sibiryak': ("Russian Cossacks and fur traders crossed the Urals in the sixteenth century and reached the Pacific within a lifetime; exiles, Old Believers, peasant settlers and Soviet builders made Siberia a land of its own, with its own toughness and humour.",
             "They pride themselves on surviving anything: pelmeni frozen by the sackful, cedar nuts, fish from the Ob and Yenisei, the banya after a winter's day, and hunting and fishing in the taiga. A Sibiryak, they say, is not someone who does not feel cold, but someone who dresses warmly."),
'tswana': ("The Sotho-Tswana chiefdoms spread across the high plateau of southern Africa; Moshoeshoe built the Basotho nation in the mountains in the nineteenth century, and Botswana's chiefs negotiated a protectorate that kept their land from the settler states.",
           "Their culture centres on cattle and the kgotla, the open council where every man may speak. Choirs and gospel, mokoro canoes in the Okavango, the Basotho blanket and conical hat, and seswaa of slow-cooked beef at weddings mark them."),
'english': ("Angles, Saxons and Jutes settled Britain in the fifth century; the Norman conquest of 1066 merged them with French-speaking lords, and England went on to give the world parliamentary government, the industrial revolution and the English language.",
            "They love the pub, tea with milk, Sunday roast and fish and chips, gardening, queuing, cricket and football. Shakespeare, the Beatles and a dry, self-deprecating humour belong to them, and so does an endless talk about the weather."),
'hausa': ("The Hausa city-states of Kano, Katsina and Zaria grew rich on trans-Saharan trade; the jihad of Usman dan Fodio in 1804 united them under the Sokoto Caliphate, and the British ruled through its emirs.",
          "Their markets are among the greatest in Africa, their language is spoken by tens of millions as a lingua franca, and their emirs still ride out in the Durbar festival on decorated horses. Tuwo with miyan kuka, suya kebabs and indigo-dyed cloth from Kano's ancient pits are theirs."),
'scandinavian': ("The Vikings of Denmark, Norway and Sweden raided, traded and settled from Newfoundland to the Volga; their Christian kingdoms later fought for mastery of the Baltic, and in the twentieth century they built some of the most equal welfare states in the world.",
                 "They light the dark winter with candles and hygge, dance round the maypole at Midsummer, eat herring, rye bread and cinnamon buns, and love nature and the right to roam it. Ibsen, Strindberg, Lego and IKEA come from them."),
'tamil': ("Tamil literature of the Sangam age is two thousand years old; the Chola empire of the tenth and eleventh centuries ruled from southern India to Sri Lanka and sent fleets to Southeast Asia, and Tamil traders and labourers settled from Malaysia to the Caribbean.",
          "They are proud of the oldest living classical literature in India, of Bharatanatyam dance and Carnatic music, of the great temples of Madurai and Thanjavur, of Pongal at the harvest, dosa, idli and sambar, filter coffee and a passionate film culture."),
'serbcroat': ("South Slavs settled the Balkans in the sixth and seventh centuries; Croatia joined the Catholic West and later Hungary, Serbia built a medieval Orthodox empire and lived under the Ottomans for centuries, and both were joined in Yugoslavia in the twentieth century, until its violent breakup.",
              "They share one language in several standards, a love of grilled cevapi, burek and rakija, coffee that takes all afternoon, kolo circle dances and the gusle epics. They argue fiercely about history and are famously hospitable to guests."),
'kannada': ("Kannada has been written since the fifth century; the Chalukya, Hoysala and Vijayanagara dynasties built the great temples of Badami, Belur and Hampi, and Mysore was a rich princely state under the Wodeyars.",
            "They celebrate Dasara in Mysore with elephants and a lit palace, love Yakshagana dance-drama and Carnatic music, eat ragi balls, bisi bele bath and Mysore pak, and turned Bengaluru into India's technology capital."),
'chilean': ("Spain conquered the central valley in the sixteenth century but never the Mapuche south; Chile won independence in 1818, took the northern deserts in the War of the Pacific, and lived through the Allende years and Pinochet's dictatorship before returning to democracy in 1990.",
            "They eat empanadas and drink wine and pisco on the Fiestas Patrias in September, dance the cueca, love their poets, Neruda and Mistral, and live between the driest desert and the stormy fjords of Patagonia."),
'pashtun': ("The Pashtuns have lived in the mountains between the Indus and the Hindu Kush for millennia; the Durrani empire founded by Ahmad Shah in 1747 created Afghanistan, and the Durand Line of 1893 split their land between Afghanistan and British India.",
            "They live by Pashtunwali, the code of honour, hospitality and protection of guests, settled in the jirga council. Attan circle dances, poetry of Rahman Baba and Khushal Khan, kabuli pulao and chapli kebab, and the strength of the extended family mark them."),
'hisp_carib': ("Hispaniola was the first Spanish colony in the Americas; the Dominican Republic and Puerto Rico grew from Spanish, African and Taino roots, and in the twentieth century millions of both peoples settled in New York and the United States.",
               "They are the people of merengue, bachata, salsa and reggaeton, of baseball as a national religion, of mangu, mofongo and arroz con habichuelas, and of loud, close families. Their Carnival masks and Christmas parrandas are famous."),
'bengali': ("Bengal was one of the richest regions of the Mughal empire; the British began their conquest of India there in 1757, and Bengal was split twice, in 1905 and in 1947, while East Bengal fought a war of independence in 1971 to become Bangladesh.",
            "They are a nation of poets and talkers: Rabindranath Tagore wrote both their national anthems, the adda of endless conversation is an institution, fish and rice are the heart of every meal, and Durga Puja and Pohela Boishakh, the new year, fill their cities."),
'african_am': ("Their ancestors were carried from West and Central Africa to North America in slavery; emancipation in 1865 was followed by Jim Crow, the Great Migration to northern cities, and the civil rights movement that changed the United States.",
               "They created the music the world dances to, spirituals, gospel, blues, jazz, soul, funk and hip hop. Soul food of collard greens, fried chicken and cornbread, the Black church, Juneteenth and Kwanzaa, and a living tradition of protest and art are theirs."),
'gujarati': ("Gujarat's ports have traded with Arabia and Africa for millennia; it was the land of the Solanki kings, the Gujarat Sultanate and then Mughal and British rule, and it gave India Mahatma Gandhi and many of its greatest merchant families.",
             "They are famous traders and travellers, vegetarians who serve sweet-savoury thali, dhokla and thepla. They dance garba and dandiya through the nine nights of Navratri, keep Diwali as their new year, and fly kites by the million at Uttarayan."),
'reacher': ("The Reachers are a new people of the far north: settlers from southern Canada, Europe and Asia who moved into the thawing Arctic of Canada in the twenty-first and twenty-second centuries, living alongside the Inuit and Dene nations.",
            "They are pioneers with their own humour and slang: long summer days of building and fishing, winter festivals with ice racing, greenhouse farms, and a respect, learned the hard way, for the knowledge of the Inuit about ice, weather and the land."),
'yoruba': ("The Yoruba built city-states like Ife, famous for its bronze and terracotta heads, and the empire of Oyo; Yoruba culture crossed the Atlantic with the slave trade and lives on in Brazil and Cuba, and Nigeria's Lagos became the greatest city of Africa.",
           "They are lovers of festivals, fine clothes and praise poetry: aso-ebi outfits at owambe parties, juju and Afrobeat music, talking drums, jollof, amala and pounded yam. The orisha of the old religion still live in their festivals alongside churches and mosques."),
'greek': ("Their history runs from Mycenae and the city-states of classical Athens and Sparta, through Alexander and the Hellenistic world, the thousand-year Byzantine empire and four centuries of Ottoman rule, to independence in 1830.",
          "They are Orthodox people of Easter midnight with red eggs and lamb on the spit, of the taverna, ouzo, olive oil, feta and grilled fish. Bouzouki and rebetiko songs, the name day, the island summer and the endless debate in the kafeneio belong to them."),
'amazonian': ("Hundreds of peoples have lived in the Amazon basin for at least ten thousand years, some in large towns and earthworks now hidden by forest; disease, rubber booms and colonisation destroyed many, but many survived in the deep forest.",
              "Their life follows the river and the forest: manioc gardens, fishing with bows and traps, hammocks, body painting with urucum and genipapo, and shamans who know thousands of plants. Each people keeps its own language, songs and myths."),
'luba': ("The Luba Kingdom of the sixteenth to nineteenth centuries was one of the great states of Central Africa, famous for its sacred kingship and lukasa memory boards; the Kasai region was later ravaged by the Congo Free State and rich in diamonds.",
         "Their carvers made some of Africa's finest sculpture, their Tshiluba is one of Congo's national languages, and their rumba and gospel fill Kinshasa. Fufu and cassava leaves, caterpillars and fish, and large family gatherings keep their traditions."),
'fulani': ("The Fula spread with their herds across the Sahel from Senegal to Sudan over a thousand years; in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries their scholars led jihads that founded the Sokoto Caliphate, Futa Jallon and Masina.",
           "They are herders above all, and the culture of the cow runs through their poetry, law and pride. The Wodaabe Gerewol beauty festival, the code of pulaaku, of patience and self-control, fura da nono millet and milk, and indigo-dyed robes mark them."),
'filipino': ("The Tagalog lands around Manila Bay became the heart of the Spanish Philippines in 1571; the Philippine Revolution of 1896 created Asia's first republic, followed by American rule and independence in 1946.",
             "They are Catholic people of fiestas and Simbang Gabi before Christmas, karaoke and basketball, adobo, sinigang and lechon. Their family ties reach across the world through millions working overseas, and bayanihan, community spirit, is their pride."),
'sindhi': ("The Indus valley of Sindh was home to Mohenjo-daro, one of the first cities on Earth; Sindh became Muslim with the Arab conquest of 712, was ruled by local dynasties and the British, and lost most of its Hindus to India at partition.",
           "They revere the Sufi saints: the shrine of Lal Shahbaz Qalandar resounds with dhamal dancing, and the poet Shah Abdul Latif's verses are sung nightly at Bhit Shah. Ajrak prints, Sindhi caps, sai bhaji and biryani belong to them."),
'zulu': ("Shaka united the Zulu clans into a military kingdom in the 1810s and 1820s; the Zulu defeated a British army at Isandlwana in 1879 before their kingdom was broken, and they lived through apartheid to the democracy of 1994.",
         "They dance the umxhentso and stomp the indlamu, sing isicathamiya in close harmony, keep the Reed Dance and the king's ceremonies, wear beadwork whose colours carry messages, and gather around umqombothi beer and grilled meat."),
'sundanese': ("The Sundanese of western Java had their own Hindu-Buddhist kingdom of Pajajaran until the sixteenth century, when Islam and the Banten sultanate took its place; Bandung later became one of the great cities of the Dutch Indies and of Indonesia.",
              "They play angklung bamboo rattles and gamelan degung, perform wayang golek with wooden puppets, love fresh vegetables with sambal, and are known in Indonesia for their gentle speech and good humour."),
'hispanic_us': ("Spanish-speaking communities have lived in the American Southwest since before it became part of the United States in 1848; twentieth-century migration from Mexico, Puerto Rico, Cuba and Central America made Hispanics the country's largest minority.",
                "They mix two worlds: quinceaneras and Thanksgiving, tejano and reggaeton, tamales at Christmas and barbecues on the Fourth of July. Spanglish, Catholic parishes, family loyalty and a growing weight in politics and culture mark them."),
'kurdish': ("The Kurds have lived in the mountains of the Zagros and Taurus for millennia; Saladin was a Kurd, Kurdish emirates flourished under the Ottomans and Safavids, and after 1918 their land was divided between Turkey, Iran, Iraq and Syria.",
            "Newroz in March, with bonfires and dancing, is their national feast. They dance the halay in long lines, sing the dengbej epics, wear colourful dress at weddings, and have the saying that the Kurds have no friends but the mountains."),
'vietnamese': ("The Kinh of the Red River delta lived under Chinese rule for a thousand years before winning independence in 938, then expanded south over the centuries to the Mekong; French colonisation was followed by thirty years of war and reunification in 1975.",
               "They celebrate Tet with banh chung and ancestral altars, eat pho, banh mi and fresh herbs with everything, honour the family and the ancestors, and love the water puppets of the north and the cai luong opera of the south."),
'rajasthani': ("Rajasthan was the land of the Rajput kingdoms, whose warrior clans fought the Delhi sultans and Mughals and later allied with them; their forts and palaces at Jaipur, Jodhpur, Udaipur and Chittorgarh are among the wonders of India.",
               "They are known for colour in the desert: turbans and odhnis, puppet shows, folk music of the Manganiyars and Langas, the camel fair at Pushkar, dal baati churma and a code of chivalry kept in ballads."),
'mesoamerican': ("The Maya built cities and a written script in the rainforest two thousand years ago, and the Nahua founded Tenochtitlan in 1325, the capital of the Aztec empire; after the Spanish conquest their peoples survived in villages, keeping languages and customs.",
                 "Maize, beans and squash still feed them; they weave and embroider huipiles, honour the dead and the saints in festivals that mix Catholic and older rites, and speak Nahuatl and dozens of Maya languages."),
'mande': ("The Mande founded the empire of Ghana and, under Sundiata Keita in the thirteenth century, the Mali Empire, whose king Mansa Musa made the most famous pilgrimage to Mecca in history; Timbuktu and Djenne became great centres of learning.",
          "The griots keep their history, singing the Sundiata epic to the kora; their mud mosques are rebuilt every year, and their music, from Salif Keita to the guitars of Bamako, is famous. Rice with peanut sauce and the bogolan mud cloth are theirs."),
'aboriginal': ("Aboriginal Australians have lived on the continent for over fifty thousand years in hundreds of nations with their own languages and laws; British colonisation from 1788 brought dispossession and violence, and the long fight for land rights followed.",
               "Their law and knowledge live in the Dreaming, carried in songlines that map the country, in ceremony, dance and painting. Dot painting, the didgeridoo of the north, bush tucker and fire management of the land are among their gifts to the world."),
'irish': ("Ireland was Celtic and Christian early, famous for its monasteries; English conquest, plantations and the Great Famine of the 1840s drove millions abroad, and the island won independence for most of its counties in 1922.",
          "They are famous for music, talk and hospitality: the session in the pub, the fiddle and the bodhran, Gaelic football and hurling, St Patrick's Day, stew and soda bread, and a literature from Joyce and Yeats to Heaney."),
'assamese': ("The Ahom kings, a Tai dynasty, ruled the Brahmaputra valley for six hundred years and repelled the Mughals; the British joined Assam to India, and the hill peoples around it kept their own tribes and languages.",
             "They celebrate Bihu three times a year with dance and drums, drink their famous strong tea, weave golden muga silk, eat fish curry with sour tenga, and revere the Vaishnava saint Sankardev and his monasteries on Majuli island."),
'chewa': ("Bantu farmers settled the lands around Lake Malawi and the Zambezi long ago; the Maravi kingdoms and later the Bemba and Lozi states rose there, followed by the Ngoni invasions, the slave trade and British rule.",
          "The Chewa keep the Nyau secret society, whose masked Gule Wamkulu dances are a world heritage; nsima of maize is eaten at every meal, and fishing on the lake, church choirs and village councils order life."),
'cree_ojibwe': ("The Cree and Ojibwe are Algonquian peoples of the boreal forest and the Great Lakes; they were partners of the fur trade, signed the numbered treaties with Canada, and survived the residential schools to rebuild their nations.",
                "They live by the land and the seasons: wild rice harvesting, moose and goose hunts, the Midewiwin lodge, birchbark canoes, dreamcatchers and syllabic writing. Powwows and the teachings of the Seven Grandfathers bind their communities."),
'turkmen': ("Oghuz Turkmen tribes lived as horse breeders in the oases and deserts east of the Caspian; they resisted Persia and then the Russian conquest, which ended with the battle of Geok Tepe in 1881, and became a Soviet republic and later an independent state.",
            "They are famous for the golden Akhal-Teke horse, the finest red carpets of Central Asia, plov and shashlyk, the wedding songs of the bagshy bards and the telpek, a tall sheepskin hat."),
'swahili': ("The Swahili city-states of Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu and Zanzibar grew from Bantu towns trading with Arabia, Persia and India across the monsoon sea; their language became the lingua franca of East Africa.",
            "They build carved wooden doors and coral-stone houses, sail dhows, eat pilau, biryani and coconut curries, sing taarab, and write poetry in Swahili, one of Africa's great literary languages."),
'quechua': ("The Quechua are heirs of the Inca empire, Tawantinsuyu, which ruled the Andes from Cusco until the Spanish conquest; Tupac Amaru II led the great rebellion of 1780, and Quechua remains the most spoken native language of the Americas.",
            "They farm potatoes and maize on ancient terraces, herd llamas and alpacas, weave bright textiles, and celebrate Inti Raymi, the festival of the sun, and the pilgrimage of Qoyllur Rit'i to a glacier under the stars."),
'javanese': ("Java was the centre of the Hindu-Buddhist Majapahit empire and of Borobudur and Prambanan; Islam spread in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, the Mataram sultanate ruled until Dutch power grew, and the Javanese became the core of Indonesia.",
             "Their culture is refined and layered: gamelan orchestras, wayang kulit shadow puppets played all night, batik, keris daggers, the courts of Yogyakarta and Solo, and a speech with different levels of politeness."),
'tibetan': ("The Tibetan empire of the seventh to ninth centuries ruled much of Inner Asia; Buddhism shaped Tibet for over a thousand years, and from the seventeenth century the Dalai Lamas ruled it, until China's annexation in 1950.",
            "Their year turns on Losar, the new year, and the great monastic festivals; they eat tsampa and drink butter tea, spin prayer wheels and fly prayer flags, and make pilgrimages around holy mountains such as Kailash."),
'sakha': ("The Sakha are Turkic people who moved north to the Lena valley centuries ago, becoming horse and cattle breeders in the coldest inhabited land on Earth; Russia absorbed them in the seventeenth century.",
          "Their great feast is Ysyakh at midsummer, with kumis and circle dances; they sing the Olonkho epic, play the khomus jaw harp, and keep horses that can survive at fifty degrees below zero."),
'niger_delta': ("The Niger Delta and the Middle Belt are home to hundreds of peoples, from the city-states of the delta that traded palm oil to the farmers of the Jos Plateau; oil, discovered in 1956, made the region the richest and most troubled part of Nigeria.",
                "Their cultures are many but share masquerades, fishing and farming festivals, pepper soup and starch, and a strong love of music, from highlife to the Afrobeats of the twenty-first century."),
'haitian': ("Haiti was the richest slave colony of the Caribbean until the slaves rose under Toussaint Louverture and won independence in 1804, the first Black republic; a long history of debt, occupation and dictatorship followed.",
            "They speak Kreyol, dance kompa, paint brilliant naive art, celebrate Carnival and Independence Day with soup joumou, and keep Vodou beside Catholic and Protestant faith."),
'scots': ("The Scots descend from Picts, Gaels, Britons and Angles; the kingdom of Scotland defended its independence in the wars against England, joined it under one crown in 1603 and one parliament in 1707, and gave the world the Scottish Enlightenment.",
          "They keep Burns Night with haggis and whisky, Hogmanay at New Year, ceilidh dances, bagpipes and tartan, and are proud inventors, engineers and philosophers."),
'guarani': ("The Paraguayans were born of Spanish settlers and Guarani people; Jesuit missions organised the Guarani in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, and Paraguay fought two devastating wars, the War of the Triple Alliance and the Chaco War.",
            "They speak Guarani as well as Spanish, drink terere, cold mate, eat chipa and sopa paraguaya, dance the polka paraguaya with the harp, and are known for nanduti lace."),
'afrikaner': ("Dutch, German and Huguenot settlers came to the Cape from 1652; the Boers trekked inland, fought the British in two wars, and ruled South Africa under apartheid until 1994. The Cape Coloured people share their language, grown from Dutch, Malay and Khoe roots.",
              "They gather around the braai, eat boerewors, bobotie and koeksisters, sing in Afrikaans, love rugby, and the Cape Malay and coloured communities keep the minstrel carnival of Cape Town."),
'cuban': ("Cuba was Spain's last great colony in the Americas, built on sugar and slavery; it won independence in 1898 and lived through the revolution of 1959 and decades of communist rule.",
          "They gave the world son, rumba, mambo, cha-cha-cha and salsa, cigars and rum, ropa vieja and moros y cristianos, and baseball played in every street. Santeria lives beside Catholic faith."),
'amazigh': ("The Amazigh are the native people of North Africa; their kings Massinissa and Jugurtha fought Rome, their dynasties, the Almoravids and Almohads, ruled Morocco and Spain, and they kept their language through every conquest.",
            "They write in Tifinagh, weave carpets with ancient symbols, wear silver jewellery, celebrate Yennayer, the Amazigh new year, and cook couscous and tagine in mountain villages."),
'evenk': ("The Evenks and Evens are Tungusic reindeer herders and hunters spread across the taiga from the Yenisei to the Pacific, over a vast area with very few people; they were drawn into Russian fur trade and Soviet collectives.",
          "Their life follows the reindeer, ridden as well as herded, and the forest: birchbark crafts, conical chum tents, shamans and their drums, and festivals at the summer gathering."),
'yemeni': ("Yemen was ancient Arabia Felix, the land of Saba and the incense trade; it became Muslim early, was ruled by imams in the north and British Aden in the south, and united in 1990.",
           "They build mud-brick tower houses, chew qat in the afternoon, wear the jambiya dagger, eat saltah and bint al-sahn, and sing in a tradition that goes back to the ancient poets of the Arabian peninsula."),
'malagasy': ("The Malagasy are descendants of Austronesian sailors from Borneo and Bantu settlers from Africa; the coastal kingdoms of the Sakalava and Betsimisaraka traded across the Indian Ocean until the Merina and then France united the island.",
             "They honour the ancestors in famadihana, the turning of the bones, eat rice three times a day, and dance and sing salegy and tsapiky."),
'somali': ("The Somali are Cushitic herders and traders of the Horn; their ports traded with Arabia and India for thousands of years, they resisted colonisation in the Dervish state of Sayyid Mohammed, and their land was divided among four colonial powers.",
           "They are a nation of poets, where poetry decides disputes. Camels, the clan, tea with cardamom, canjeero pancakes and rice with banana, and a far-flung diaspora mark them."),
'inuit': ("The Inuit spread from Alaska to Greenland about a thousand years ago and mastered the Arctic: they hunted seals, walrus and whales from kayaks and umiaks, and lived in snow houses on the ice.",
          "They carve soapstone, play throat-singing games, build inuksuit cairns, wear parkas of sealskin and caribou, and keep the knowledge of ice and weather that made life possible in the High Arctic."),
}
